.TH b4-standing-orders-list 1  "b4 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
b4\-standing\-orders\-list \- List standing orders
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBb4 standing\-orders list\fR [\fB\-\-account\fR] [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-header\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
List standing orders
.PP
List the standing orders stored in the vault, ordered by account: id, account, payee, purpose, the amount with its currency (negative is money leaving the account), the interval, the day it runs on, the next execution day and the state.
.PP
The day is the day of the month, or of the week for a weekly order; 97 to 99 stand for days counted from the end of the month. The next execution day is known only for orders that are kept in Banking4 (state `local`). For an order held at the bank (state `at_bank`) Banking4 computes it when it shows the order, and b4 does not: the field is empty.
.PP
With \-\-output json, jsonl or csv every standing order has these fields, named as in `b4 schema standing_orders`: account_id, id, account_name, scheme, amount, currency, debit, payee_name, payee_iban, payee_bic, purpose, category, state, is_local, period_unit, period_length, execution_day, first_execution_date, last_execution_date, next_execution_date, stored_next_execution_date.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-account\fR \fI<ACCOUNT>\fR
Only standing orders of this account

ACCOUNT is tried in this order: the numeric id from `b4 accounts list`; the IBAN (blanks and case do not matter); the account number; a part of the account name (case does not matter) that only one account has, where a complete name wins over a part. If several accounts match, the command fails and lists them with their ids.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<PATH>\fR [default: ./b4.duckdb]
Database file: written by `decrypt`, read by every other command
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBB4_DB\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
Output format [default: table]

table is made for reading: line breaks, control characters and text direction overrides in the data are replaced. csv, jsonl and json are data: written to a file or a pipe they are exact, byte for byte. Only when they are written to a terminal are the characters a terminal acts on (escape sequences, text direction overrides) made harmless: csv shows U+FFFD in their place, json and jsonl write them as \\uXXXX escapes.

In json and jsonl a number that a double cannot hold exactly is a string of its digits: an integer beyond 2^53, a decimal of more than 15 significant digits.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
table: Aligned text, fitted to the terminal
.IP \(bu 2
json: One JSON array of objects
.IP \(bu 2
jsonl: One JSON object per line
.IP \(bu 2
csv: Comma\-separated values with a header line
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-header\fR
Leave out the header line of table and csv output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Do not print informational messages to stderr

Silences progress and summary lines and the "no rows" note. Warnings and errors are still printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
b4 standing\-orders list
    List the standing orders of all accounts.
b4 standing\-orders list \-\-account giro
    Only those of one account.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBb4\fR(1),
\fBb4\-standing\-orders\fR(1)
